Dear Martin

Unit 4, Weir Lane – the lease plan

I attach the landlord's lease plan for the estate (drawing WL/04/01, August 2026). It will be fixed to the lease, so it needs to be right before you sign. Please look closely at four things, and tell me if anything differs from what you were shown when you viewed the unit:

1. the boundary of the unit's yard, which is hatched on the plan;
2. the loading bay in front of it;
3. parking spaces 7 and 8, which are shaded and are the only spaces the lease gives you; and
4. the bin store, which the plan puts beside the loading bay.

You should also know where the lease stands. Cardew Hollis, the landlord's solicitors, have answered our changes. The landlord has accepted £45,000 a year with the first three months rent-free, and a break at the end of year five on six months' notice. In return it wants two things: a rent deposit of £13,500 (a quarter's rent of £11,250, plus VAT), and a schedule of condition of the unit, made at your cost, so that your repairing duty is measured against how the unit looks now.

Helen will write to you with a full report next week, once the landlord's agents have sent the estate's service charge budget. If you would like to talk before then, I am on 01632 960216.

